{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba \"$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/pose-mcp/internal/cli/review_reap_test.go | sed -n '\"'1,180p'; nl -ba \\\"\"'$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/pose-mcp/internal/cli/delegate.go | sed -n '\"'60,130p'; nl -ba \\\"\"'$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/pose-mcp/internal/cli/delegate.go | sed -n '\"'1110,1155p'; nl -ba \\\"\"'$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_release.go | sed -n '\"'150,260p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t//go:build linux\n     2\t\n     3\tpackage cli\n     4\t\n     5\timport (\n     6\t\t\"os\"\n     7\t\t\"os/exec\"\n     8\t\t\"path/filepath\"\n     9\t\t\"strconv\"\n    10\t\t\"strings\"\n    11\t\t\"syscall\"\n    12\t\t\"testing\"\n    13\t\t\"time\"\n    14\t\n    15\t\t\"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/pose\"\n    16\t)\n    17\t\n    18\t// Spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R1, R2, R4: a\n    19\t// reviewer that ends while a command it started in a session of its own is\n    20\t// still running leaves a run that completes, with that command ended before\n    21\t// the cleanup and named in the run record.\n    22\tfunc TestDispatchEndsWhatTheReviewerLeftRunning(t *testing.T) {\n    23\t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"setsid\"); err != nil {\n    24\t\t\tt.Skip(\"setsid is not available\")\n    25\t\t}\n    26\t\troot := dispatchInstance(t)\n    27\t\tpidFile := filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), \"leftover.pid\")\n    28\t\tscript := `cat >/dev/null; setsid sh -c 'echo $$ > \"` + pidFile + `\"; while :; do touch \"$TMPDIR/leftover\" 2>/dev/null; sleep 0.05; done' </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 & ` +\n    29\t\t\t`i=0; while [ ! -s \"` + pidFile + `\" ] && [ $i -lt 100 ]; do sleep 0.05; i=$((i+1)); done; printf 'decision: approved\\n'`\n    30\t\twriteReviewers(t, root, map[string]pose.ReviewerAdapter{\"fake\": {Command: []string{\"sh\", \"-c\", script}, Vendor: \"fixture\", Model: \"fixture\", Containment: &pose.ReviewerContainment{SchemaVersion: 1, Profile: \"trusted-process\"}}})\n    31\t\tcode, out := runPose(t, root, \"delegate\", \"review\", \"spec:alpha\", \"--agent\", \"fake\")\n    32\t\traw, err := os.ReadFile(pidFile)\n    33\t\tif err != nil {\n    34\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the leftover never started: %v (%d %s)\", err, code, out)\n    35\t\t}\n    36\t\tpid, _ := strconv.Atoi(strings.TrimSpace(string(raw)))\n    37\t\tt.Cleanup(func() { _ = syscall.Kill(pid, syscall.SIGKILL) })\n    38\t\tdeadline := time.Now().Add(2 * time.Second)\n    39\t\tfor syscall.Kill(pid, 0) == nil && time.Now().Before(deadline) {\n    40\t\t\tif stat, err := os.ReadFile(\"/proc/\" + strconv.Itoa(pid) + \"/stat\"); err == nil && strings.Contains(string(stat), \") Z \") {\n    41\t\t\t\tbreak\n    42\t\t\t}\n    43\t\t\ttime.Sleep(50 * time.Millisecond)\n    44\t\t}\n    45\t\tif stat, err := os.ReadFile(\"/proc/\" + strconv.Itoa(pid) + \"/stat\"); err == nil && !strings.Contains(string(stat), \") Z \") {\n    46\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the process the reviewer left running survived the run: %s\", stat)\n    47\t\t}\n    48\t\truns, _, _ := (pose.Store{Root: root}).ReadReviewRuns(\"\")\n    49\t\tif len(runs) != 1 {\n    50\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"runs: %d (%d %s)\", len(runs), code, out)\n    51\t\t}\n    52\t\trun := runs[0]\n    53\t\tif run.Status != pose.ReviewRunCompleted || run.Safety == nil || run.Safety.Operation.Outcome != pose.OutcomeCompleted {\n    54\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the run did not complete with its cleanup: status=%s failure=%s safety=%+v\", run.Status, run.Failure, run.Safety)\n    55\t\t}\n    56\t\tif !strings.Contains(strings.Join(run.Safety.Limitations, \"\\n\"), \"process(es) the run left running\") {\n    57\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the run record does not name the ended process: %v\", run.Safety.Limitations)\n    58\t\t}\n    59\t}\n    60\t\n    61\ttype delegateFlags struct {\n    62\t\tagent, access, label, lead, reason, noteFile string\n    63\t\trequest                                      string\n    64\t\tto, cd, effort, promptFile, session          string\n    65\t\taddDirs                                      []string\n    66\t\tnoNetwork, record, confirmAbandoned          bool\n    67\t\tdryRun, jsonOut, active, wait, detach        bool\n    68\t\tverbose, noFollow                            bool\n    69\t\tlines                                        int\n    70\t\twaitMax, timeout, refresh                    time.Duration\n    71\t\tpositional                                   []string\n    72\t}\n    73\t\n    74\t// parseDelegateFlags reads the common flags and the extra ones a subcommand\n    75\t// takes; problem is a message for the person, in their locale.\n    76\tfunc parseDelegateFlags(args []string, extra ...string) (delegateFlags, string) {\n    77\t\tf := delegateFlags{waitMax: 24 * time.Hour}\n    78\t\tpermit := map[string]bool{}\n    79\t\tfor _, name := range append(append([]string(nil), commonDelegateFlags...), extra...) {\n    80\t\t\tpermit[name] = true\n    81\t\t}\n    82\t\tfor i := 0; i < len(args); i++ {\n    83\t\t\targ := args[i]\n    84\t\t\tname, inline, hasInline := strings.Cut(arg, \"=\")\n    85\t\t\t// A lone - names stdin (spec pose-delegation-task R1).\n    86\t\t\tif arg == \"-\" || !strings.HasPrefix(arg, \"-\") {\n    87\t\t\t\tf.positional = append(f.positional, arg)\n    88\t\t\t\tcontinue\n    89\t\t\t}\n    90\t\t\tif !permit[name] {\n    91\t\t\t\treturn f, tr(\"unknown or inapplicable flag \"+name, \"flag desconhecida ou inaplicável \"+name)\n    92\t\t\t}\n    93\t\t\tswitch name {\n    94\t\t\tcase \"--dry-run\", \"--json\", \"--active\", \"--detach\", \"-v\", \"--no-follow\", \"--no-network\", \"--record\", \"--confirm-abandoned\":\n    95\t\t\t\tif hasInline {\n    96\t\t\t\t\treturn f, tr(name+\" takes no value\", name+\" não recebe valor\")\n    97\t\t\t\t}\n    98\t\t\t\tswitch name {\n    99\t\t\t\tcase \"--dry-run\":\n   100\t\t\t\t\tf.dryRun = true\n   101\t\t\t\tcase \"--json\":\n   102\t\t\t\t\tf.jsonOut = true\n   103\t\t\t\tcase \"--detach\":\n   104\t\t\t\t\tf.detach = true\n   105\t\t\t\tcase \"-v\":\n   106\t\t\t\t\tf.verbose = true\n   107\t\t\t\tcase \"--no-network\":\n   108\t\t\t\t\tf.noNetwork = true\n   109\t\t\t\tcase \"--record\":\n   110\t\t\t\t\tf.record = true\n   111\t\t\t\tcase \"--confirm-abandoned\":\n   112\t\t\t\t\tf.confirmAbandoned = true\n   113\t\t\t\tcase \"--no-follow\":\n   114\t\t\t\t\tf.noFollow = true\n   115\t\t\t\tdefault:\n   116\t\t\t\t\tf.active = true\n   117\t\t\t\t}\n   118\t\t\tcase \"--wait-until-reset\":\n   119\t\t\t\tf.wait = true\n   120\t\t\t\tif hasInline {\n   121\t\t\t\t\td, err := time.ParseDuration(inline)\n   122\t\t\t\t\tif err != nil || d <= 0 || d > 24*time.Hour {\n   123\t\t\t\t\t\treturn f, tr(\"--wait-until-reset takes a duration up to 24h\", \"--wait-until-reset recebe uma duração de até 24h\")\n   124\t\t\t\t\t}\n   125\t\t\t\t\tf.waitMax = d\n   126\t\t\t\t}\n   127\t\t\tdefault:\n   128\t\t\t\tvalue := inline\n   129\t\t\t\tif !hasInline {\n   130\t\t\t\t\t// --prompt - reads stdin (spec pose-delegation-task R8).\n  1110\t}\n  1111\t\n  1112\tfunc cmdDelegateCancel(root string, args []string, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int {\n  1113\t\tf, problem := parseDelegateFlags(args, \"--confirm-abandoned\")\n  1114\t\tif problem != \"\" {\n  1115\t\t\treturn delegateUsage(stdout, stderr, args, \"cancel\", problem+\"\\n\"+tr(\"Usage: \", \"Uso: \")+\"pose delegate cancel <run> [--confirm-abandoned] [--dry-run] [--json]\")\n  1116\t\t}\n  1117\t\tstore := posemodel.Store{Root: root}\n  1118\t\tattempts, lost, err := store.ListDelegationAttempts()\n  1119\t\tif err != nil {\n  1120\t\t\treturn delegateFail(stdout, stderr, f.jsonOut, \"cancel\", 1, err)\n  1121\t\t}\n  1122\t\tattempt, code, err := namedAttempt(attempts, f)\n  1123\t\tif err != nil {\n  1124\t\t\treturn delegateFail(stdout, stderr, f.jsonOut, \"cancel\", code, withDelegationLosses(err, lost))\n  1125\t\t}\n  1126\t\tif attempt.State == posemodel.DelegationStalled && attempt.LatestRun == \"\" && !f.dryRun {\n  1127\t\t\t// An attempt whose dispatch ended before recording its run is\n  1128\t\t\t// released with a failed run and its receipt, so it stops blocking\n  1129\t\t\t// the bundle (spec pose-review-run-reaps-lingering-reviewer-processes R5).\n  1130\t\t\trun, err := store.ReleaseAbandonedAttempt(attempt.AttemptID, time.Now(), f.confirmAbandoned)\n  1131\t\t\tif err != nil {\n  1132\t\t\t\treturn delegateFail(stdout, stderr, f.jsonOut, \"cancel\", 1, err)\n  1133\t\t\t}\n  1134\t\t\tif f.jsonOut {\n  1135\t\t\t\twriteDelegateJSON(stdout, map[string]any{\"schema\": delegateCancelSchema, \"attempt\": attempt.AttemptID, \"released\": true, \"run\": run.RunID, \"status\": run.Status})\n  1136\t\t\t\treturn 0\n  1137\t\t\t}\n  1138\t\t\tout := render(stdout, stderr)\n  1139\t\t\tout.Field(\"delegate.attempt\", attempt.AttemptID)\n  1140\t\t\tif run.RunID == \"\" {\n  1141\t\t\t\tout.Field(\"delegate.released\", tr(\"true: no reservation was published; the stalled marker was removed\", \"true: nenhuma reserva foi publicada; o marcador parado foi removido\"))\n  1142\t\t\t} else {\n  1143\t\t\t\tout.Field(\"delegate.released\", tr(\"true: recorded as failed run \"+run.RunID+\" (abandoned before its run was recorded)\", \"true: registrada como run com falha \"+run.RunID+\" (abandonada antes de o run ser registrado)\"))\n  1144\t\t\t}\n  1145\t\t\treturn 0\n  1146\t\t}\n  1147\t\tif !attempt.Active {\n  1148\t\t\treturn delegateFail(stdout, stderr, f.jsonOut, \"cancel\", 1, withDelegationLosses(errors.New(tr(\"attempt \"+attempt.AttemptID+\" is \"+attempt.State+\", not running\",\n  1149\t\t\t\t\"a tentativa \"+attempt.AttemptID+\" está \"+attempt.State+\", não rodando\")), lost))\n  1150\t\t}\n  1151\t\tif f.dryRun {\n  1152\t\t\tif f.jsonOut {\n  1153\t\t\t\twriteDelegateJSON(stdout, map[string]any{\"schema\": delegateCancelSchema, \"attempt\": attempt.AttemptID, \"requested\": false, \"dry_run\": true, \"owner\": attempt.Liveness})\n  1154\t\t\t\treturn 0\n  1155\t\t\t}\n   150\t\t\trun.Kind, run.Purpose, run.Label, run.Round, run.StartedAt = DelegationKindForPurpose(marker.Purpose), marker.Purpose, marker.Label, marker.Round, marker.StartedAt\n   151\t\t\trun.Failure = DelegationAbandonedCause + \" (stalled at \" + marker.StalledAt + \"); released by pose delegate cancel\"\n   152\t\t} else {\n   153\t\t\trun.Failure = DelegationAbandonedCause + \" (reservation of \" + reserved.ReservedAt + \" with no marker, left by an earlier engine); released by pose delegate cancel on the operator's confirmation\"\n   154\t\t}\n   155\t\t// A release interrupted after recording the run completes with that run.\n   156\t\trecorded, found, err := s.recordedAbandonmentRun(*reserved)\n   157\t\tif err != nil {\n   158\t\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, err\n   159\t\t}\n   160\t\tif !found {\n   161\t\t\tif recorded, err = s.recordReviewRun(run, nil); err != nil {\n   162\t\t\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, err\n   163\t\t\t}\n   164\t\t}\n   165\t\tif err := s.finishDelegatedReviewAttempt(*reserved, recorded); err != nil {\n   166\t\t\treturn recorded, err\n   167\t\t}\n   168\t\tif marker != nil {\n   169\t\t\treturn recorded, s.removeDelegationMarker(*marker)\n   170\t\t}\n   171\t\treturn recorded, nil\n   172\t}\n   173\t\n   174\t// recordedAbandonmentRun finds the failed run an interrupted release of a\n   175\t// recorded for it.\n   176\tfunc (s Store) recordedAbandonmentRun(a DelegatedReviewAttempt) (ReviewRun, bool, error) {\n   177\t\truns, _, err := s.ReadReviewRuns(a.BundleID)\n   178\t\tif err != nil {\n   179\t\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, false, err\n   180\t\t}\n   181\t\tfor _, r := range runs {\n   182\t\t\tif r.AttemptID == a.AttemptID && r.Status == ReviewRunFailed && strings.HasPrefix(r.Failure, DelegationAbandonedCause) {\n   183\t\t\t\treturn r, true, nil\n   184\t\t\t}\n   185\t\t}\n   186\t\treturn ReviewRun{}, false, nil\n   187\t}\n   188\t\n   189\t// orphanReservations lists the reservations an earlier engine left without\n   190\t// a receipt, a marker or a run, older than reviewOrphanReservationAge, and\n   191\t// says which reservations or receipts it could not read (review round 3, N3).\n   192\tfunc (s Store) orphanReservations(now time.Time) ([]DelegatedReviewAttempt, []string, error) {\n   193\t\tbase := filepath.Join(s.Root, reviewAttemptDir)\n   194\t\tdirs, err := os.ReadDir(base)\n   195\t\tif errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n   196\t\t\treturn nil, nil, nil\n   197\t\t}\n   198\t\tif err != nil {\n   199\t\t\treturn nil, nil, err\n   200\t\t}\n   201\t\tmarkers, _, err := s.ListDelegationActive()\n   202\t\tif err != nil {\n   203\t\t\treturn nil, nil, err\n   204\t\t}\n   205\t\tmarked := map[string]bool{}\n   206\t\tfor _, m := range markers {\n   207\t\t\tmarked[m.AttemptID] = true\n   208\t\t}\n   209\t\truns, _, err := s.ReadReviewRuns(\"\")\n   210\t\tif err != nil {\n   211\t\t\treturn nil, nil, err\n   212\t\t}\n   213\t\tran := map[string]bool{}\n   214\t\tfor _, r := range runs {\n   215\t\t\tran[r.AttemptID] = true\n   216\t\t}\n   217\t\tvar out []DelegatedReviewAttempt\n   218\t\tvar lost []string\n   219\t\tbudget := reviewReadBudget(nil)\n   220\t\tfor _, d := range dirs {\n   221\t\t\tif !d.IsDir() {\n   222\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   223\t\t\t}\n   224\t\t\tentries, err := os.ReadDir(filepath.Join(base, d.Name()))\n   225\t\t\tif err != nil {\n   226\t\t\t\tlost = append(lost, \"the reservations in \"+d.Name()+\" could not be listed: \"+err.Error())\n   227\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   228\t\t\t}\n   229\t\t\tfor _, e := range entries {\n   230\t\t\t\tif IsPublisherStaging(e.Name()) || !strings.HasSuffix(e.Name(), \".json\") || strings.HasSuffix(e.Name(), \".result.json\") {\n   231\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   232\t\t\t\t}\n   233\t\t\t\tvar a DelegatedReviewAttempt\n   234\t\t\t\tif err := readLedgerJSONBudgetIn(s.fsys(), filepath.Join(base, d.Name(), e.Name()), &a, budget); err != nil {\n   235\t\t\t\t\tlost = append(lost, \"the reservation \"+d.Name()+\"/\"+e.Name()+\" could not be read: \"+err.Error())\n   236\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   237\t\t\t\t}\n   238\t\t\t\tif a.AttemptID != attemptIdentity(a) {\n   239\t\t\t\t\tlost = append(lost, \"the reservation \"+d.Name()+\"/\"+e.Name()+\" does not match its identity\")\n   240\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   241\t\t\t\t}\n   242\t\t\t\tif marked[a.AttemptID] || ran[a.AttemptID] {\n   243\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   244\t\t\t\t}\n   245\t\t\t\tif _, err := os.Lstat(attemptReceiptPath(s.Root, a)); err == nil {\n   246\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   247\t\t\t\t} else if !errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n   248\t\t\t\t\tlost = append(lost, \"the receipt of \"+a.AttemptID+\" could not be inspected: \"+err.Error())\n   249\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   250\t\t\t\t}\n   251\t\t\t\tat, err := time.Parse(time.RFC3339Nano, a.ReservedAt)\n   252\t\t\t\tif err != nil || now.Sub(at) < reviewOrphanReservationAge {\n   253\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n   254\t\t\t\t}\n   255\t\t\t\tout = append(out, a)\n   256\t\t\t}\n   257\t\t}\n   258\t\treturn out, lost, nil\n   259\t}\n   260\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'confirm-abandoned|owner.*confirmed|no owner record|ten minutes|ten-minute|operator.*confirmation' \\\"\"'$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/POSE.md \"$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md \"$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md \"$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md; rg -n '\"'ReviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands|brief.*finish|review-brief/10' \\\"\"'$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/pose-mcp/internal/pose/testdata/review_brief_template_digests.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'confirm-abandoned|owner.*confirmed|no owner record|ten minutes|ten-minute|operator.*confirmation' \\\"\"'$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/POSE.md \"$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md \"$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md \"$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md; rg -n '\"'ReviewBriefTemplateFinishCommands|brief.*finish|review-brief/10' \\\"\"'$POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT\"/pose-mcp/internal/pose/testdata/review_brief_template_digests.json'","aggregated_output":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2823723771/worktree/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md:376:- `review bundle` / `review attest` / `review auto-attest` / `review verify` / `review record` — review de ponto fixo com opt-in (`review_bundles: true` e data de adoção). A preparação resume Intent, Requirements, Technical Plan e Decisions sem incluir Tasks, logs de execução, Final Report, lifecycle, atestações ou estado derivado no digest. `--seal` persiste JSON imutável no diretório review-bundles sob uma identidade `rvb-`; atestações ficam em JSON separado e append-only no diretório review-attestations sob uma identidade `rva-`. `auto-attest` extrai evidências correspondentes dos resultados de validação e resolve disposições de ferramentas deterministicamente. Mudanças semânticas ou de fonte criam um bundle sucessor e delta tipado; mudanças derivadas de closeout não exigem nova revisão. O fluxo local é offline. O Conductor pode devolver um envelope Ed25519 opcional somente quando o pin de emissor/chave estiver confiado pela policy. A saída humana agrupa avisos repetidos e separa tools obrigatórias ativas, recomendadas e adiadas para conclusão; o JSON preserva a proveniência completa. O selo também registra os contratos de governança em vigor (`component-aware`, `review-bundles`, `evidence-vocabulary`) e os gates que decidem o bundle (reservas, severidades de risco aceito, reuso de critério); a verificação os lê do bundle, e só `require_signed_attestations` é lido ao vivo. O selo avisa sobre evidência produzida contra um commit diferente do head aprovado. O selo também registra a identidade da implementação: principal, vendor, modelo, um digest da referência da sessão que selou (nunca a referência) e se ela foi `detected` pelos marcadores de agente da sessão ou `declared` com `--seal --lead <vendor>[/<modelo>]`. O modelo vem de `--lead` ou de `routing.lead_models` e fica `unknown` caso contrário, o que nunca estabelece diferença de modelo; marcadores de dois vendors deixam o vendor `unknown`. Selar de novo um conteúdo que um bundle selado já contém devolve esse bundle com a identidade que ele registrou, de modo que um revisor fechando a spec nunca vira a implementação; só um `--lead` que nomeia uma identidade diferente da do bundle vigente sela de novo, e esse selo nomeia o bundle que substitui, de modo que governa independentemente dos instantes de selo; um run comparado com uma identidade substituída deixa de valer. A identidade é selada, mas não é conteúdo: um bundle selado corresponde a uma preparação que difere dele só pela identidade. Um critério `passed` de uma attestation precisa citar evidência que o bundle contém, de uma classe que o critério aceita (qualquer uma de suas `evidence_classes`), do componente de que ele trata — evidência de um diretório dentro de um componente não responde pelo componente. `--criterion ID|disposition|evidence|rationale` registra `passed`, `not-applicable` (exige rationale) ou `finding` (nomeia um finding registrado); `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]` exige severidade e ação, e `accepted-risk` ou `wont-fix` exige uma severidade aceita no selo, um dono, um rationale e uma data de revisão. A avaliação é um DAG, e cada etapa nomeia a anterior: o subject de implementação carrega um `implementation_digest` sobre o seu conteúdo classificado apenas — não sobre ids de change set nem refs, de modo que squash ou rebase do mesmo conteúdo preservam a identidade — e quem observa a implementação se ancora ali, não no bundle que vai contê-la. A evidência selada registra `subject_observation` como `observed`, `carried-forward` ou `unknown`; um resultado que não nomeia commit é `unknown`, nunca atual. A observação de range de um change set informa quantos commits `base..head` abrange contra quantos ele atribui, porque os paths resolvidos por trailers são atribuídos enquanto o intervalo entre eles é apenas proveniência; ela fica fora do payload selado, então um commit não relacionado nunca torna obsoleta a revisão de um conteúdo que não se moveu. Um criterion é `mechanical` quando nomeia classes de evidência que um check registrado emite, e `judgment` caso contrário: `auto-attest` responde o primeiro tipo a partir da evidência selada e reporta o segundo como pendência, recusando `--apply` até que um revisor registre uma conclusão para cada um. Um profile pode declarar `kind` explicitamente para exigir julgamento sobre um criterion que um check também cobre; não pode declarar `mechanical` em um que não nomeia classe de evidência. Uma tool com escopo de componente cujo componente a matriz de validação declara não rodar check algum (`replaceDefaultChecks` com lista vazia) é planejada com `producer_coverage: none` e pode ser disposta `not-used` com um motivo, porque nada no repositório consegue emitir o que ela pede; em qualquer outro caso uma tool obrigatória ainda precisa passar. MCP read-only: `pose_review_bundle`. `pose_review_prepare` (read-only) devolve o rascunho do auto-attest e os critérios de julgamento pendentes; `pose_review_attest` (spec `pose-mcp-review-attest-signed-only`, `governance-write`) registra uma attestation pelo MCP só dentro do envelope de um emissor confiável, a prova que `--envelope` verifica; o preview completa um rascunho para um bundle selado (digest do bundle, horário, id e, quando nomeia `confirmed_by`, o digest da confirmação) e devolve os bytes exatos a assinar. Uma confirmação humana aparece como verificada só quando a claim de autoridade assinada nomeia esse humano.\n/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2823723771/worktree/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1075:**Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** No Linux, depois que o adaptador termina, o envio também encerra todo processo cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), o que pega comandos que o revisor iniciou em sessão própria; cada um é fixado por um pidfd e conferido de novo antes do sinal. Ele espera que terminem e os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza, declarando ali os processos do mesmo usuário anteriores ao run cujo ambiente não pôde ser lido (um iniciado durante o run que não pode ser lido derruba a limpeza); nas outras plataformas só o grupo de processos é encerrado; o brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. O envio marca a tentativa como ativa antes de publicar a reserva, então um envio que termina antes de registrar o run sempre deixa uma tentativa parada, que `pose delegate ls` lista com a causa, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo; uma liberação roda por vez, e uma interrompida é assumida e concluída. Uma reserva que um motor anterior deixou sem marcador não registra dono: `pose delegate ls` a lista depois de dez minutos, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa> --confirm-abandoned` a libera com a confirmação do operador de que nenhum envio dela está rodando.\n/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2823723771/worktree/POSE.md:372:- `review bundle` / `review attest` / `review auto-attest` / `review verify` / `review record` — opt-in fixed-point review (`review_bundles: true` plus an adoption date). Evaluation is a DAG, and each stage names the one before it: the implementation subject carries an `implementation_digest` over its classified content alone — not over change-set ids or refs, so a squash or rebase of the same content keeps its identity — and an observer of the implementation anchors there rather than on the bundle that will contain it. Sealed evidence records `subject_observation` as `observed`, `carried-forward` or `unknown`; a result naming no commit is unknown, never current. A change set's range observation reports how many commits `base..head` spans against how many it attributes, because paths resolved from trailers are attributed while the range between them is only provenance; it sits outside the sealed payload, so an unrelated commit never stales a review of content that did not move. A criterion is `mechanical` when it names evidence classes a registered check emits, and `judgment` otherwise: `auto-attest` answers the first kind from the sealed evidence and reports the second as pending, refusing `--apply` until a reviewer records a conclusion for each. A profile may declare `kind` explicitly to ask for judgment on a criterion a check also covers; it may not declare `mechanical` on one naming no evidence class. A component-scoped tool whose component the validation matrix declares runs no check (`replaceDefaultChecks` with an empty list) is planned with `producer_coverage: none` and may be disposed `not-used` with a reason, because nothing in the repository can emit what it asks for; everywhere else a required tool must still pass. Preparation hashes the semantic Intent, Requirements, Technical Plan and Decisions, attributed patch/tree content, consumed plan inputs, validation identities and child bundles. Tasks, execution logs, Final Report, lifecycle metadata, attestations and derived state remain outside the review digest but retain their own gates. `--seal` persists immutable JSON in the review-bundles directory under an `rvb-` identity; attestations are separate append-only JSON in review-attestations under an `rva-` identity. `auto-attest` extracts matching evidence from validation results and resolves tool dispositions deterministically. Semantic or source changes create a superseding bundle and typed delta; derived closeout changes do not cause rereview. Local review stays offline. Conductor may return an optional Ed25519 envelope only when its issuer/public-key pin is trusted by policy. Human plan output groups repeated path warnings and separates active required, recommended and completion-deferred tools; JSON retains full provenance. Sealing also records the governance contracts in force (`component-aware`, `review-bundles`, `evidence-vocabulary`) and the gates that decide the bundle (reservations, accepted-risk severities, criterion reuse); verification reads them from the bundle, and only `require_signed_attestations` is read live. Sealing warns about evidence produced against a commit other than the approved head. Sealing also records the implementation identity: principal, vendor, model, a digest of the sealing session's reference (never the reference) and whether it was `detected` from the session's agent markers or `declared` with `--seal --lead <vendor>[/<model>]`. The model comes from `--lead` or `routing.lead_models` and is `unknown` otherwise, which never establishes a model difference; markers of two vendors leave the vendor `unknown`. Resealing content a sealed bundle already holds returns that bundle with the identity it recorded, so a reviewer closing out never becomes the implementation; only a `--lead` naming another identity than the governing bundle's seals anew, and that seal names the bundle it replaces, so it governs whatever the seal instants say; a run compared with a replaced identity stops governing. The identity is sealed but is not content: a sealed bundle matches a preparation that differs from it only by the identity. An attestation's `passed` criterion must cite evidence the bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts (any one of its `evidence_classes`), from the component it is about — evidence from a directory inside a component does not answer for the component. `--criterion ID|disposition|evidence|rationale` records `passed`, `not-applicable` (rationale required) or `finding` (names a recorded finding); `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]` needs a severity and an action, and `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` needs a sealed accepted severity, an owner, a rationale and a review date. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_bundle`. `pose_review_prepare` (read-only) returns the auto-attest draft and its pending judgment criteria; `pose_review_attest` (spec `pose-mcp-review-attest-signed-only`, `governance-write`) records an attestation over MCP only inside a trusted issuer's envelope, the proof `--envelope` verifies; its preview completes a draft for a sealed bundle (bundle digest, attested time, id and, when it names `confirmed_by`, the confirmation digest) and returns the exact bytes to sign. A human confirmation is disclosed as verified only when the signed authority claim names that human.\n/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2823723771/worktree/POSE.md:380:- `artifact-check` — parses exact `### Artifacts` actions, resolves an explicit base/head range or `POSE-Spec: <slug>` commit trailers with safe structured Git arguments, and reports resolvability, existence, action mismatch, undeclared and orphan findings. A claim on a changelog fragment that `pose release prepare` archived resolves through the release manifest, which records the fragment's spec and digest; prepare never edits a spec. A file a later spec removes needs no longer exist for the earlier specs that declare it `created` or `modified`: their claim is satisfied — an edge `removed-by` in `delivery-integrity.json` — when the later spec declares `removed:` for the path, its attributed change sets contain a commit that deleted it (a rename is not a deletion), and every change the earlier spec made to the path — a merge, a root creation, a type change or a deletion included — is a Git ancestor of that commit; a change after the removal, a removal its spec never performed, a change set naming something other than an immutable commit, or history Git cannot read — a shallow clone included; replace refs and grafts are ignored — keeps the existence finding, and the finding says why. Commits modifying artifacts declared by a spec must carry a `POSE-Spec: <slug>` trailer in their commit message for `artifact-check` and `pose close` to attribute the changes. `pose report --change-from/--change-to` persists immutable change-set evidence; `pose index` projects claims, observations, reverse provenance and findings into `delivery-integrity.json`. The seven files `pose index` writes are one generation (spec `pose-index-generation-integrity`): their bytes are written under `generations/<id>/` in the indexes directory, the legacy names are published from the same bytes, and the descriptor `generation.json` there — SHA-256 of each file plus the digests of the inputs they were computed from — is published last and is the commit. A reader fixes the generation the descriptor names and checks each file against it: a set that does not verify (a file missing, replaced, from another generation, or a descriptor it cannot read) is `unavailable`, with the reason, never an empty or mixed result; a repository that never ran `pose index` since this contract keeps working and is reported `legacy-unversioned`, with no claim of coherence. A generated index edited by hand has no effect while the generation's copy exists (readers read the generation), and in a clone without the copy it makes the set unavailable; either way `pose index --verify` names the file. `pose index --verify` reads without writing and exits 1 unless the set is current, the legacy names are those of the generation and its inputs (specs, roadmaps, policy, module metadata, validation matrix) still hold. A failed publication leaves the previous generation in force and a retry converges on the same identity; publishers hold a generation lock in the indexes directory (it never waits: a second `pose index` is refused with `index-generation-busy` and writes nothing, and a lock whose holder is proven dead is recovered), the first publication of a repository is marked pending so one that did not commit reads as `unavailable` and not as a mixture of two runs, an operation that reads several indexes (the review plan) fixes one generation for all of them, and a commit whose durability could not be confirmed is reported as committed; retention keeps the current generation and the newest others up to three, never anything younger than ten minutes, and a retention failure is a warning, not a failed publication. Limits: Git history is read by the delivery graph but not pinned by the descriptor, and a clone without the git-ignored `generations/` directory verifies the legacy names against the descriptor. MCP: `pose_delivery_integrity`.\n/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2823723771/worktree/POSE.md:1078:**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux, after the adapter exits, the dispatch also ends every process whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), which catches commands a reviewer started in a session of its own; each is pinned by a pidfd and checked again before it is signalled. It waits for them and lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory, and discloses there the processes of the same user that predate the run and whose environment could not be read (one started during the run that cannot be read fails the cleanup); on other platforms only the process group is ended; the brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, and an interrupted one is taken over and completed. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\n/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2823723771/worktree/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:372:- `review bundle` / `review attest` / `review auto-attest` / `review verify` / `review record` — opt-in fixed-point review (`review_bundles: true` plus an adoption date). Evaluation is a DAG, and each stage names the one before it: the implementation subject carries an `implementation_digest` over its classified content alone — not over change-set ids or refs, so a squash or rebase of the same content keeps its identity — and an observer of the implementation anchors there rather than on the bundle that will contain it. Sealed evidence records `subject_observation` as `observed`, `carried-forward` or `unknown`; a result naming no commit is unknown, never current. A change set's range observation reports how many commits `base..head` spans against how many it attributes, because paths resolved from trailers are attributed while the range between them is only provenance; it sits outside the sealed payload, so an unrelated commit never stales a review of content that did not move. A criterion is `mechanical` when it names evidence classes a registered check emits, and `judgment` otherwise: `auto-attest` answers the first kind from the sealed evidence and reports the second as pending, refusing `--apply` until a reviewer records a conclusion for each. A profile may declare `kind` explicitly to ask for judgment on a criterion a check also covers; it may not declare `mechanical` on one naming no evidence class. A component-scoped tool whose component the validation matrix declares runs no check (`replaceDefaultChecks` with an empty list) is planned with `producer_coverage: none` and may be disposed `not-used` with a reason, because nothing in the repository can emit what it asks for; everywhere else a required tool must still pass. Preparation hashes the semantic Intent, Requirements, Technical Plan and Decisions, attributed patch/tree content, consumed plan inputs, validation identities and child bundles. Tasks, execution logs, Final Report, lifecycle metadata, attestations and derived state remain outside the review digest but retain their own gates. `--seal` persists immutable JSON in the review-bundles directory under an `rvb-` identity; attestations are separate append-only JSON in review-attestations under an `rva-` identity. `auto-attest` extracts matching evidence from validation results and resolves tool dispositions deterministically. Semantic or source changes create a superseding bundle and typed delta; derived closeout changes do not cause rereview. Local review stays offline. Conductor may return an optional Ed25519 envelope only when its issuer/public-key pin is trusted by policy. Human plan output groups repeated path warnings and separates active required, recommended and completion-deferred tools; JSON retains full provenance. Sealing also records the governance contracts in force (`component-aware`, `review-bundles`, `evidence-vocabulary`) and the gates that decide the bundle (reservations, accepted-risk severities, criterion reuse); verification reads them from the bundle, and only `require_signed_attestations` is read live. Sealing warns about evidence produced against a commit other than the approved head. Sealing also records the implementation identity: principal, vendor, model, a digest of the sealing session's reference (never the reference) and whether it was `detected` from the session's agent markers or `declared` with `--seal --lead <vendor>[/<model>]`. The model comes from `--lead` or `routing.lead_models` and is `unknown` otherwise, which never establishes a model difference; markers of two vendors leave the vendor `unknown`. Resealing content a sealed bundle already holds returns that bundle with the identity it recorded, so a reviewer closing out never becomes the implementation; only a `--lead` naming another identity than the governing bundle's seals anew, and that seal names the bundle it replaces, so it governs whatever the seal instants say; a run compared with a replaced identity stops governing. The identity is sealed but is not content: a sealed bundle matches a preparation that differs from it only by the identity. An attestation's `passed` criterion must cite evidence the bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts (any one of its `evidence_classes`), from the component it is about — evidence from a directory inside a component does not answer for the component. `--criterion ID|disposition|evidence|rationale` records `passed`, `not-applicable` (rationale required) or `finding` (names a recorded finding); `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]` needs a severity and an action, and `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` needs a sealed accepted severity, an owner, a rationale and a review date. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_bundle`. `pose_review_prepare` (read-only) returns the auto-attest draft and its pending judgment criteria; `pose_review_attest` (spec `pose-mcp-review-attest-signed-only`, `governance-write`) records an attestation over MCP only inside a trusted issuer's envelope, the proof `--envelope` verifies; its preview completes a draft for a sealed bundle (bundle digest, attested time, id and, when it names `confirmed_by`, the confirmation digest) and returns the exact bytes to sign. A human confirmation is disclosed as verified only when the signed authority claim names that human.\n/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2823723771/worktree/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:380:- `artifact-check` — parses exact `### Artifacts` actions, resolves an explicit base/head range or `POSE-Spec: <slug>` commit trailers with safe structured Git arguments, and reports resolvability, existence, action mismatch, undeclared and orphan findings. A claim on a changelog fragment that `pose release prepare` archived resolves through the release manifest, which records the fragment's spec and digest; prepare never edits a spec. A file a later spec removes needs no longer exist for the earlier specs that declare it `created` or `modified`: their claim is satisfied — an edge `removed-by` in `delivery-integrity.json` — when the later spec declares `removed:` for the path, its attributed change sets contain a commit that deleted it (a rename is not a deletion), and every change the earlier spec made to the path — a merge, a root creation, a type change or a deletion included — is a Git ancestor of that commit; a change after the removal, a removal its spec never performed, a change set naming something other than an immutable commit, or history Git cannot read — a shallow clone included; replace refs and grafts are ignored — keeps the existence finding, and the finding says why. Commits modifying artifacts declared by a spec must carry a `POSE-Spec: <slug>` trailer in their commit message for `artifact-check` and `pose close` to attribute the changes. `pose report --change-from/--change-to` persists immutable change-set evidence; `pose index` projects claims, observations, reverse provenance and findings into `delivery-integrity.json`. The seven files `pose index` writes are one generation (spec `pose-index-generation-integrity`): their bytes are written under `generations/<id>/` in the indexes directory, the legacy names are published from the same bytes, and the descriptor `generation.json` there — SHA-256 of each file plus the digests of the inputs they were computed from — is published last and is the commit. A reader fixes the generation the descriptor names and checks each file against it: a set that does not verify (a file missing, replaced, from another generation, or a descriptor it cannot read) is `unavailable`, with the reason, never an empty or mixed result; a repository that never ran `pose index` since this contract keeps working and is reported `legacy-unversioned`, with no claim of coherence. A generated index edited by hand has no effect while the generation's copy exists (readers read the generation), and in a clone without the copy it makes the set unavailable; either way `pose index --verify` names the file. `pose index --verify` reads without writing and exits 1 unless the set is current, the legacy names are those of the generation and its inputs (specs, roadmaps, policy, module metadata, validation matrix) still hold. A failed publication leaves the previous generation in force and a retry converges on the same identity; publishers hold a generation lock in the indexes directory (it never waits: a second `pose index` is refused with `index-generation-busy` and writes nothing, and a lock whose holder is proven dead is recovered), the first publication of a repository is marked pending so one that did not commit reads as `unavailable` and not as a mixture of two runs, an operation that reads several indexes (the review plan) fixes one generation for all of them, and a commit whose durability could not be confirmed is reported as committed; retention keeps the current generation and the newest others up to three, never anything younger than ten minutes, and a retention failure is a warning, not a failed publication. Limits: Git history is read by the delivery graph but not pinned by the descriptor, and a clone without the git-ignored `generations/` directory verifies the legacy names against the descriptor. MCP: `pose_delivery_integrity`.\n/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2823723771/worktree/pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:1065:**Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux, after the adapter exits, the dispatch also ends every process whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), which catches commands a reviewer started in a session of its own; each is pinned by a pidfd and checked again before it is signalled. It waits for them and lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory, and discloses there the processes of the same user that predate the run and whose environment could not be read (one started during the run that cannot be read fails the cleanup); on other platforms only the process group is ended; the brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, and an interrupted one is taken over and completed. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\n/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2823723771/worktree/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md:376:- `review bundle` / `review attest` / `review auto-attest` / `review verify` / `review record` — review de ponto fixo com opt-in (`review_bundles: true` e data de adoção). A preparação resume Intent, Requirements, Technical Plan e Decisions sem incluir Tasks, logs de execução, Final Report, lifecycle, atestações ou estado derivado no digest. `--seal` persiste JSON imutável no diretório review-bundles sob uma identidade `rvb-`; atestações ficam em JSON separado e append-only no diretório review-attestations sob uma identidade `rva-`. `auto-attest` extrai evidências correspondentes dos resultados de validação e resolve disposições de ferramentas deterministicamente. Mudanças semânticas ou de fonte criam um bundle sucessor e delta tipado; mudanças derivadas de closeout não exigem nova revisão. O fluxo local é offline. O Conductor pode devolver um envelope Ed25519 opcional somente quando o pin de emissor/chave estiver confiado pela policy. A saída humana agrupa avisos repetidos e separa tools obrigatórias ativas, recomendadas e adiadas para conclusão; o JSON preserva a proveniência completa. O selo também registra os contratos de governança em vigor (`component-aware`, `review-bundles`, `evidence-vocabulary`) e os gates que decidem o bundle (reservas, severidades de risco aceito, reuso de critério); a verificação os lê do bundle, e só `require_signed_attestations` é lido ao vivo. O selo avisa sobre evidência produzida contra um commit diferente do head aprovado. O selo também registra a identidade da implementação: principal, vendor, modelo, um digest da referência da sessão que selou (nunca a referência) e se ela foi `detected` pelos marcadores de agente da sessão ou `declared` com `--seal --lead <vendor>[/<modelo>]`. O modelo vem de `--lead` ou de `routing.lead_models` e fica `unknown` caso contrário, o que nunca estabelece diferença de modelo; marcadores de dois vendors deixam o vendor `unknown`. Selar de novo um conteúdo que um bundle selado já contém devolve esse bundle com a identidade que ele registrou, de modo que um revisor fechando a spec nunca vira a implementação; só um `--lead` que nomeia uma identidade diferente da do bundle vigente sela de novo, e esse selo nomeia o bundle que substitui, de modo que governa independentemente dos instantes de selo; um run comparado com uma identidade substituída deixa de valer. A identidade é selada, mas não é conteúdo: um bundle selado corresponde a uma preparação que difere dele só pela identidade. Um critério `passed` de uma attestation precisa citar evidência que o bundle contém, de uma classe que o critério aceita (qualquer uma de suas `evidence_classes`), do componente de que ele trata — evidência de um diretório dentro de um componente não responde pelo componente. `--criterion ID|disposition|evidence|rationale` registra `passed`, `not-applicable` (exige rationale) ou `finding` (nomeia um finding registrado); `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]` exige severidade e ação, e `accepted-risk` ou `wont-fix` exige uma severidade aceita no selo, um dono, um rationale e uma data de revisão. A avaliação é um DAG, e cada etapa nomeia a anterior: o subject de implementação carrega um `implementation_digest` sobre o seu conteúdo classificado apenas — não sobre ids de change set nem refs, de modo que squash ou rebase do mesmo conteúdo preservam a identidade — e quem observa a implementação se ancora ali, não no bundle que vai contê-la. A evidência selada registra `subject_observation` como `observed`, `carried-forward` ou `unknown`; um resultado que não nomeia commit é `unknown`, nunca atual. A observação de range de um change set informa quantos commits `base..head` abrange contra quantos ele atribui, porque os paths resolvidos por trailers são atribuídos enquanto o intervalo entre eles é apenas proveniência; ela fica fora do payload selado, então um commit não relacionado nunca torna obsoleta a revisão de um conteúdo que não se moveu. Um criterion é `mechanical` quando nomeia classes de evidência que um check registrado emite, e `judgment` caso contrário: `auto-attest` responde o primeiro tipo a partir da evidência selada e reporta o segundo como pendência, recusando `--apply` até que um revisor registre uma conclusão para cada um. Um profile pode declarar `kind` explicitamente para exigir julgamento sobre um criterion que um check também cobre; não pode declarar `mechanical` em um que não nomeia classe de evidência. Uma tool com escopo de componente cujo componente a matriz de validação declara não rodar check algum (`replaceDefaultChecks` com lista vazia) é planejada com `producer_coverage: none` e pode ser disposta `not-used` com um motivo, porque nada no repositório consegue emitir o que ela pede; em qualquer outro caso uma tool obrigatória ainda precisa passar. MCP read-only: `pose_review_bundle`. `pose_review_prepare` (read-only) devolve o rascunho do auto-attest e os critérios de julgamento pendentes; `pose_review_attest` (spec `pose-mcp-review-attest-signed-only`, `governance-write`) registra uma attestation pelo MCP só dentro do envelope de um emissor confiável, a prova que `--envelope` verifica; o preview completa um rascunho para um bundle selado (digest do bundle, horário, id e, quando nomeia `confirmed_by`, o digest da confirmação) e devolve os bytes exatos a assinar. Uma confirmação humana aparece como verificada só quando a claim de autoridade assinada nomeia esse humano.\n/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-2823723771/worktree/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1075:**Runs que deixam trabalho para trás.** No Linux, depois que o adaptador termina, o envio também encerra todo processo cujo ambiente nomeia o checkout selado do run (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`), o que pega comandos que o revisor iniciou em sessão própria; cada um é fixado por um pidfd e conferido de novo antes do sinal. Ele espera que terminem e os lista em `safety.limitations` do run antes do inventário de limpeza, declarando ali os processos do mesmo usuário anteriores ao run cujo ambiente não pôde ser lido (um iniciado durante o run que não pode ser lido derruba a limpeza); nas outras plataformas só o grupo de processos é encerrado; o brief do template `review-brief/10` pede ao revisor que deixe seus comandos terminarem. O envio marca a tentativa como ativa antes de publicar a reserva, então um envio que termina antes de registrar o run sempre deixa uma tentativa parada, que `pose delegate ls` lista com a causa, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa>` a libera quando o dono está comprovadamente encerrado, registrando um run com falha e o seu recibo; uma liberação roda por vez, e uma interrompida é assumida e concluída. Uma reserva que um motor anterior deixou sem marcador não registra dono: `pose delegate ls` a lista depois de dez minutos, e `pose delegate cancel <tentativa> --confirm-abandoned` a libera com a confirmação do operador de que nenhum envio dela está rodando.\n3:  \"review-brief/10\": \"sha256:7bc73ce9b27c5360350867235a0a458ff96c4409f14df58cfec0caa64d3992a4\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
